yielding a base triple plus prerelease flag. The go directive has 4 total classes and NO forbidden class - a value with a path separator is unclassifiable, not package influence, stated explicitly. The toolchain directive has 7 total ordered classes: path-bearing and custom-distribution (go1.23.4-bigcorp) are forbidden, default is permitted and never honored, canonical release and prerelease names compare by base triple, catch-all is mismatch; a repeated directive is the catch-all. Base-triple canonicalization is exact rather than approximate: Go orders 1.21 < 1.21rc1 < 1.21.0, and the two orders can differ only for a comparand strictly between a language version and its release, every such value being a prerelease that Stage A already rejected. go.work is deliberately not a metadata source because protocol/core.md 4.2 fixes GOWORK=off. Grammar taken from on-host primary sources (modfile ToolchainRE/GoVersionRE, gover.FromToolchain), not from memory. Supporting rule: new 3.1.1 value classifiers for a field whose value space spans dispositions - ordered classes, forbidden first, mandatory catch-all.\n\n(2) Overlapping triggers partitioned, not ordered. Wire surface: unknown key is the existing unknown-field schema rejection, malformed value is build_toolchain_requirement_invalid, so package_influence_forbidden never fires on the wire and is a Stage B code only. Rationale: classifying a malformed literal as smuggled influence requires inferring intent from a byte string and two managers would infer differently. The field-addition prohibition moves to a release gate over published schema property names, owned by TASK-260728-2jaw7h, because a field that does not exist produces no value to diagnose. Stage A step 2 splits into 2a declaration / 2b origin trust / 2c shape: unavailable means nothing was declared and is produced only by 2a; untrusted means something was declared and is unusable, including a declared root that does not exist. The cut is declaration presence, n